(2) 4.1.2 CONSISTENCY ACROSS BROWSER LOCALES — PASS
The divergence this attempt closes: `aria-valuetext` and the Go-rendered display span are
built server-side by hand-rolled `formatNumber`/`formatDollars` (internal/templates/
render.go:546, a manual digit-grouping loop with a hardcoded `,` — no OS/browser locale
input at all, so it always emits "7,387"-style grouping regardless of server locale).
Before this attempt, the JS-side re-computation on every value change called bare
`.toLocaleString()`, which resolves to the *browser's* locale — on a de-DE or fr-FR
browser this would render "7.387" or "7 387", producing a live divergence between what
the aria-valuetext announces (always the en-US-style string, since aria-valuetext is set
from the same JS `formatWholeDollars` — after this fix, always 'en-US' — but before this
fix, if a stray path used a locale-default call, the two could visibly disagree) and what
the display span shows on non-US browsers. Now every JS call in scope routes through one
of: `formatWholeDollars` (single definition, quick-adjust-scripts.html:10-12, pinned
`'en-US'`) or a directly-pinned `.toLocaleString('en-US', ...)`. Since the primary
control's aria-valuetext (portfolio-settings.html:181, quick-adjust.html mirror) and its
live-sync value (quick-adjust-scripts.html's syncQuickAdjustKey, `exactValueText =
formatWholeDollars(...)`) both go through the same now-pinned function, they agree
byte-for-byte REGARDLESS of the browser's locale — a browser set to de-DE, ar-EG, or any
other locale now produces the identical "$7,387" string an en-US browser would, matching
the server's initial paint exactly. Re-ran `TestMonthlyLivingExpensesWholeDollarRounding_
HalfAwayFromZeroTie` (unaffected by locale — Node's `toLocaleString('en-US')` doesn't vary
with the test-runner's OS locale) — still PASS at the .50 tie, confirming the fix didn't
regress the rounding rule itself, only pinned the digit-grouping/decimal-separator choice.

(3) HEALTHCARE-PERSON HANDLERS — locale pin only, no AT-announcement change in default
locale — PASS
These three range inputs (current_monthly_cost, aca_cost_after_employer,
medicare_monthly_cost) carry no `aria-valuetext` (confirmed: grep for aria-valuetext in
healthcare-person.html returns nothing) — their AT-exposed value is the native implicit
aria-valuenow, read straight off the `value` attribute (an integer set server-side via
`value="{{printf ...}}"`, never touched by JS or by any toLocaleString call). The
oninput-updated sibling `<span data-quick-adjust-display=...>` is plain sighted/AT-visible
text but is not wired to the control via aria-describedby/aria-labelledby — it was already
an OBSERVATION-level gap (label association) at attempt 3, unchanged here, out of scope
for W2. Because Go's `formatNumber` is always en-US-style grouping and the JS call is now
pinned to `'en-US'` too, the string produced for a `toLocaleString('en-US')` call is
mathematically identical to the previous bare `toLocaleString()` call FOR A BROWSER
ALREADY RUNNING IN en-US — a locale environment guaranteed identical output before and
after, so there is zero change in what any locale-en-US session (screen reader or sighted)
sees or is announced for these three fields. The only behavior change is for non-en-US
browsers, where the display now matches the server-rendered figure it previously could
diverge from — a fix, not a regression. Re-verified `go build`/`go vet`/full `go test`
green (above) confirms no server-side handler logic changed for these fields (handlers.go
diff for this attempt's window shows no touch to healthcare handling; the handlers.go
edit under this manifest is entirely attempt-<=3 content per the mtime split above).

